{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If they fill</b>. By way of parable, concerning wealth that was given to the rich only to do good for all who depend on him." "<b>And the word יהוא</b>. It is plural, and the alef is added like the alef of ההלכוא אתו ולא אבוא שמוע, for at first it is יהי in the singular, and it has no parallel in its grammatical pattern in all of Scripture. The sense concerns the place where the fruit of the tree falls, whether north or south — there the gatherers will be. And the matter concerns the rich. And some say it is about the tree itself, and this half-verse corresponds to the first half of the verse, and its latter half corresponds to the latter half. And this commentator said that the yod of יהוא is future of the word הוא, and it is singular, and the sense is that the tree, because it gives fruit, they irrigate it and work it, and when it falls they know it will never give fruit, and they abandon it where it fell. And one commentator said that this tree is from counsel, and the one who says thus did not eat from the tree of knowledge."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "The quoted form 'ההלכוא אתו ולא אבוא שמוע' is left untranslated as Ibn Ezra cites it as a morphological parallel; the phrase appears to be Aramaic-like and is textually obscure. The closing phrase 'והאומר כזה לא אכל מעץ הדעת' is rendered hyper-literally; it appears to be a sharp dismissive metaphor but its exact referent is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:12.035642442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}